          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em gauge theory
            may denote either a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            or a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+trajectories"} field configurations
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} abelian
            or
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Weil+theory"} nonabelian
          %h3#ordinary_gauge_theories Ordinary gauge theories
          %p
            An
            %em ordinary gauge theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            whose field configurations are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
          %p
            This includes notably the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            that carry the three fundamental forces of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Ordinary
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
                in the absence of magnetic charges is a gauge theory of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+U%281%29-bundles"} principal U(1)-bundles
                with
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %li
              %p
                Fields in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
                (such as appearing in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
                and in
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUTs
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundles
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
          %p Other examples include formal physical models.
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
              is a gauge theory whose field configurations are
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundles
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              a finite group (these come with a unique
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
          %p
            The group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            in these examples is called the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of the theory.
          %h3#higher_and_generalized_gauge_theories Higher and generalized gauge theories
          %p
            The above examples of gauge fields consisted of cocycles in degree-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            More generally, a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+theory"} higher gauge theory
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            whose field configurations are cocycles in more general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycles
            or more generally cocycles in other differential refinements, such as in differential
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
          %p This generalization does contain experimentally visible physics such as
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+current"} magnetic current
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetism"} electromagnetism
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe with connection
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
              refining a cocycle in degree-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane cohomology
              %em magnetic charge
              \.
          %p
            But a whole tower of higher and generalized gauge theories became visible with the study of higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            theories,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
                is a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe with connection
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
                with curvature 3-form.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cocycle
                with curvature 4-form.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                is a cocycle in
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
          %h3#gravity_as_a_nongauge_theory Gravity as a (non-)gauge theory
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+formulation+of+gravity"} first order formulation of gravity
            also the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            looks a little like a gauge theory. However, there is a crucial difference. What really happens here is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            field, namelely an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+structure"} orthogonal structure
            on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsion+of+a+G-structure"} torsion freedom
            of this G-structure may be encoded by an auxiliary
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            gravity is described by many of the ingredients from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            that also govern pure gauge theory, it’s not quite the same. In particular there is a constraint on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+connection"} Cartan connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vielbein"} vielbein
            fields is the constraint that the vielbein (which is part of the Cartan connection) is non-degenerate, and hence really a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soldering+form"}> soldering form
            ”. Such a constraint is absent in a “genuine” gauge theory such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#nonredundancy_and_locality Non-redundancy and locality
          %p
            Sometimes one see the view expressed that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetry"} gauge symmetry
            is “just a redundancy” in the description of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+%28physics%29"} theory of physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            it is only the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+invariance"} gauge invariant
            ones which are physically meaningful.
          %p This statement however
          %h3#anomalies Anomalies
          %p
            In the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+charge"} magnetic charge
            (and then even in the absence of
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                chiral fermion anomalies
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/chiral+fermion+anomaly"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"} Green-Schwarz mechanism
            is a famous phenomenon in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            by which such a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancels against that given by chiral fermions.
